hexagon: htp: keep the sign of zero in the f16 conversion of v79, copy to f16 without unroll
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-htp

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

hvx_vec_f32_to_f16_shuff converts f32 to f16 through a qf32 value. On v79 it
makes that value with an add of 0, which gives +0 for -0, and the CPU
conversion keeps -0. In the v79 simulator this is the only difference from the
CPU conversion over 862208 values (each exponent, each 10-bit head, the ties
and their neighbors) and 25 special values. On v81 the function gives the CPU
bits. The app graphs reach this condition where a K or V value, a Q value of
flash attention or an activation is -0: its f16 value is +0, which gives the
same products.

This patch makes the qf32 value with a multiply by 1.0 on v79, which keeps
-0. Each other value keeps its bits.

The patch also removes the unroll(4) pragma of the f32 -> f16 copy loop
(hvx_copy_f16_f32_*). The bits do not change. In the simulator the loop
without the pragma costs about a third fewer cycles on v75, v79 and v81 (v79:
411 in place of 621 cycles for one unaligned copy of 2560 values, 91 in place
of 126 for 512). The loop is used by SET_ROWS to f16 (the KV cache writes), CPY
from f32 to f16, the Q and output conversions of flash attention, the
activations of the flat matmul, and im2col.

On the phone (OnePlus 13, the 2B Q8_0 on HTP0, 2026-09-24, the runs in the order
old, new, old, new): tg32 at depth 0 gives 23.40 and 23.32 t/s without the patch and
23.28 and 23.51 with it, tg32 at depth 4096 gives 22.77 and 22.61 against 22.83 and
22.84, and pp512 gives 1945 and 1951 against 1944 and 1960. The decode KL against the
naive oracle is 0.000618 without the patch and 0.000657 with it, both at the floor. In
the op profile of the decode at depth 4096, SET_ROWS takes 2.0 % and CPY 2.7 % fewer
cycles, and the other ops do not change.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-base.h b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-base.h
index d7575b9..99c8e0e 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-base.h
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-base.h
@@ -121,10 +121,18 @@ static inline HVX_VectorPred hvx_vec_is_nan_f16(HVX_Vector v) {
     return Q6_Q_and_QQ(p_exp, p_frac);
 }
 
+// The f16 values of two f32 vectors. On v79 and v81 the result is the IEEE conversion with round to
+// nearest even, the conversion of the CPU backend. On v79 the multiply by 1.0 keeps the sign of a
+// zero, where an add of 0 gives +0 for -0. Before v79 the qf32 to hf conversion rounds a tie up,
+// and it gives a NaN for an Inf (tools/htp-lab, target f16rne).
 static inline HVX_Vector hvx_vec_f32_to_f16_shuff(HVX_Vector v0, HVX_Vector v1) {
 #if __HVX_ARCH__ >= 81
     HVX_Vector q0 = Q6_Vqf32_equals_Vsf(v0);
     HVX_Vector q1 = Q6_Vqf32_equals_Vsf(v1);
+#elif __HVX_ARCH__ >= 79
+    const HVX_Vector one = hvx_vec_splat_f32(1.0f);
+    HVX_Vector q0 = Q6_Vqf32_vmpy_VsfVsf(v0, one);
+    HVX_Vector q1 = Q6_Vqf32_vmpy_VsfVsf(v1, one);
 #else
     const HVX_Vector zero = Q6_V_vzero();
     HVX_Vector q0 = Q6_Vqf32_vadd_VsfVsf(v0, zero);
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-copy.h b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-copy.h
index a3e33c3..2698ad5 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-copy.h
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-copy.h
@@ -148,6 +148,8 @@ static inline void hvx_copy_f32_uu(uint8_t * restrict dst, const uint8_t * restr
 
 //// fp32 -> fp16
 
+// The loop has no unroll pragma. With unroll(4) it costs about 50 % more cycles in the simulator
+// of v75, v79 and v81 (v79: 621 in place of 413 cycles for one unaligned copy of 2560 values).
 #define hvx_copy_f16_f32_loop_body(dst_type, src_type, vec_store)                   \
     do {                                                                            \
         dst_type * restrict vdst = (dst_type *) dst;                                \
@@ -160,7 +162,6 @@ static inline void hvx_copy_f32_uu(uint8_t * restrict dst, const uint8_t * restr
                                                                                     \
         uint32_t i = 0;                                                             \
                                                                                     \
-        _Pragma("unroll(4)")                                                        \
         for (; i < nvec; i++) {                                                     \
             vdst[i] = hvx_vec_f32_to_f16(vsrc[i*2+0], vsrc[i*2+1]);                 \
         }                                                                           \
